# 0010 — libvmaf on the two Windows builds, via msys2/PKGBUILD/40-mingw-w64-libvmaf.
#
# WINDOWS, coarse, and that is the honest answer rather than the convenient one. Both windows
# targets get the filter: msys2/build.sh and msys2/buildarm64.sh share the PKGBUILD directory and
# each gained the same --enable-libvmaf line. docs/verification-gate.md says to reach for the fine
# tier only when a patch builds for one target and not its sibling, which is not the case here —
# contrast 0008, which genuinely is linux64-only.
#
# ONE line, not two. 0008 declares both `libvmaf` and `libvmaf_cuda` because on linux the CUDA half
# can silently no-op (configure:7392 is check_pkg_config, not require_). There is no such risk here:
# `libvmaf_cuda` is not built on Windows at all and must NOT be, since nvcc needs MSVC that clang64
# does not have. Declaring it would assert a feature this patch deliberately does not ship.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE. It proves the filter is PRESENT, not that it SCORES THE SAME as
# media-01 — and parity is the entire reason the patch exists, since eta's numbers are meant to be
# comparable with the Linux scorer's. Nothing a GPU-less runner can ask ffmpeg will establish that.
# The standing check is a by-hand cross-host measurement recorded in
# docs/patches/0010-libvmaf-windows.md; re-run it after any vmaf version bump on either side.

filter  windows  libvmaf
